- Never commit raw reference/source PDFs under `[SET AT SETUP: LARGE_BINARY_EXCLUDE_PATHS]`
  (gitignored — see repository layout in `AGENTS.md`).
- Never commit a generated large binary (report PDF, checkpoint, firmware
  image, capture file) as a plain git object — route it through git LFS
  (`.gitattributes` tracks `[SET AT SETUP: LFS_TRACKED_PATTERNS]`); extend
  the patterns before adding a new kind of binary.
- **Understand what LFS does and does not do.** It does *not* keep only the
  latest version of a file — it stores **every** committed version in LFS
  storage, merely moving the bytes out of the packfile so clones stay fast.
  Nothing is pruned automatically, and most hosts enforce an LFS quota per
  namespace, so:
  - Commit generated artifacts at **milestones**, not on every rebuild. A
    PDF or binary regenerated on each iteration consumes quota permanently.
  - If an artifact genuinely only ever needs its latest version, do not
    version it at all — publish it as a release asset or CI artifact.
  - Pruning old LFS objects after the fact requires history rewriting,
    which "never force-push" above forbids. Prevention is the only cheap
    option; treat each LFS commit as permanent.
