## Branching

**Work is committed on a non-`main` branch; `main` only receives reviewed,
user-approved work.** The commit guard (`command_guard.py`) auto-allows
`git commit` on any branch that is not `main`/`master`, so each role
records its own pass as it finishes without an approval prompt. On `main`,
and on a detached HEAD, `git commit` stays `force_ask`. Merge, push,
checkout and switch stay `force_ask` on every branch — how work reaches
`main` or leaves the machine is the user's decision.

**Loops share a branch:** all loops share one working tree, and so one checked-out
branch; every role commits only its own paths (see "Who records, and
when"). Roles never create, switch or merge branches. The user picks the
branch, and the Controller raises it when a loop's work looks like it
wants its own (e.g. before starting a large, separable body of work).

**Why this exists.** In the project this rule came from, a sprint closed twelve Reviewer-verified
directives into a single commit: `git commit` needed approval on `main`,
nobody was present, and the loop ran on without recording anything, so no
directive could be reverted or bisected on its own.

## Remote

`origin` → `[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE]` (SSH, private repo). Commits happen
per-role as work finishes (see "Who records, and when"); pushing to `origin`
is a separate, visible action — confirm with the user before pushing unless
a given session has already authorized it for that pass. **The push, not the
commit, is what makes work visible outside this machine**, so it is the
point at which the Reviewer's gate matters: do not push a directive that has
not been reviewed and closed.
